## What a plan is
One JSON object with these keys, every line short and editable by a non-technical owner:
- "name": the app's name, 1 to 3 words, Title Case.
- "data": one line per record type, `Label · field, field (detail)`. id = the record type: lowercase snake_case, singular. Name the fields the owner mentioned, add the few a real tool needs (status, owner, dates, notes), and give the choices of any status in order: `status (Open → In progress → Done)`, using the owner's words, else the usual stages for that kind of work (sales: Lead, Qualified, Proposal, Won, Lost). A date with a time of day is one field: `starts (date and time)`. Say what a field points at when it links records: `company (a Company)`. Every record type needs a name or title field. Three to five types at most.
- "automation": one line per run, saying when it fires and what it does. id = run id: lowercase kebab-case. Only what the owner asked for, or one clearly useful run; none is fine, `[]`. A summary or answer the owner reads is not a run: the agent gives it from a button on a screen, so say that on the screen's line. Anything that sends goes "you approve before it sends"; never plan an email nobody asked for.
- "screens": one line per screen. id = screen id: lowercase kebab-case. One screen per main record type, named for it in the plural (`deals`), showing a list with a New button and the selected record in a panel to read, edit and delete. A type with a status the owner works through also gets a board on its screen; a subset the owner asked to see (running low, upcoming, overdue) is named in that screen's line and shown above its list; add one extra screen only for a summary the owner asked for. At most 6; one navigation row joins them.
- "agent": `{"line": "Name agent · what it does with content"}`, no id.
- "needs": the literal string "computed". codeaf fills it from app.json; never list needs yourself.
Each plan line: at most 120 characters, one short clause; detail goes in the files, not the plan line. Plain words, no jargon, no file names. Ids are stable once chosen; Write names the files after them. Plan only what the kit catalog can build. Hard editing (slides, documents, sheets, timelines, graphs, maps) uses exactly one surface part. Anything the catalog cannot build (phone or browser access, guests outside the workspace, a public marketplace, live co-typing) is left out, not faked.

